yoloctl local patch 0003: restore the workload calls OpenShell v0.0.72 allowed.

OpenShell's child hardening filter (openshell-sandbox
src/linux/child_seccomp.rs, prepare) denies these calls, which the v0.0.72
workload filter (openshell-supervisor-process src/sandbox/linux/seccomp.rs)
never denied. That broke GNU make (posix_spawn with POSIX_SPAWN_RESETIDS
calls setgid/setuid(own id)), nice, ionice, killpg and process-group test
suites inside every box. Restored, as in v0.0.72:

- unconditionally allowed again: capset, setuid, setgid, setreuid, setregid,
  setresuid, setresgid, setfsuid, setfsgid, setgroups, ioprio_set
- prlimit64, sched_setaffinity, sched_setattr, sched_setparam,
  sched_setscheduler and setpriority: since v0.1.10 (#5212) upstream runs a
  caller-only call (pid 0) natively and sends a named target to the broker
  (src/linux/process_sched.rs), which allows only the caller's own thread
  group. yoloctl's broker rule allows any target except the trusted
  sandbox's thread group, and setpriority also with PRIO_PGRP and PRIO_USER.
  The child filter still denies the sandbox leader as defense in depth
- allowed again with PID zero or a negative PID (process groups, killpg),
  except a signal to the sandbox's own process group (-sandbox TGID):
  kill, rt_sigqueueinfo

The sandbox-targeted denies stay because the sandbox's own startup probe
(openshell-sandbox src/main.rs, prlimit sandbox / process-group signal)
requires them; they also protect the trusted sandbox from the workload.
The workload has no capabilities and runs with no_new_privs, so the kernel
still refuses any real identity or capability gain. Every deny v0.0.72 also
had is kept, and the other denies (signals to the sandbox's own TGID,
F_SETOWN, sethostname, ...) are unchanged.

